# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto

# Rust sources are checked out as LF wherever the clone happens, whatever
# `core.autocrlf` the machine that clones carries: rustfmt writes LF, git stores LF,
# and a worktree whose files disagree with that is one `cargo fmt` rewrites on sight.
# CI checks out the same bytes, so a formatting run answers the same there as here.
*.rs text eol=lf

# Hooks are shell scripts that git runs itself, and a shebang with a carriage return
# on the end is not a shebang: a Windows clone carrying `core.autocrlf` would rewrite
# these to CRLF and every hook would fail with `bad interpreter` before running a
# line. Same reason as the rule above, and the same fix - say the line ending out
# rather than leaving it to whichever machine is cloning.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
